<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Appaliuna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Appaliuna was a god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilusa">Wilusa</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, named among the divine witnesses of the treaty between the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaksandu">Alaksandu</a> of Wilusa around 1280 BCE. Some scholars connect the name with the Greek god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, who in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> is a protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, though the link is debated.</p></div>
